node3 from its own pool with itself as default gateway\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"eth2 set to 10.50.10.254/24; Kea DHCPv4 configured with pool 10.50.10.100-.200, default-router 10.50.10.254, subnet-id 1, service confirmed active. node3's eth1 (via a new NetworkManager DHCP profile) obtained 10.50.10.100/24 with default route 10.50.10.254 and successfully pinged the gateway. Lease file on node1 shows node3's MAC bound to 10.50.10.100. Confirmed again after a full reboot of both node1 and node3, with node3 automatically reacquiring the same lease/route without executor intervention, demonstrating persistence.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b4a400e15e524ab3\",\"cmd-1fd1daeae8564444\",\"cmd-72f250b7c19f42a4\",\"cmd-509b364e46514d3b\",\"cmd-3de8cfeec0504404\",\"cmd-25cf4295411548bf\",\"cmd-ce2f6fcefe8345fd\",\"cmd-3d9d4b41b7c94485\"]},{\"id\":\"node2-dhcp-lan-b\",\"requirement\":\"node2 (OPNsense) serves lan-b addresses to node4 from its own pool with itself as default gateway\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"opt2/vtnet2 set to 10.60.10.254/24; Kea DHCPv4 subnet 10.60.10.0/24 configured with pool 10.60.10.100-.200 and router option 10.60.10.254, generated into kea-dhcp4.conf and running. A pre-existing factory dnsmasq service squatting UDP/67 was diagnosed (DHCPSRV_OPEN_SOCKET_FAIL) and permanently disabled via config.xml + template reload so Kea could bind. node4's eth1 obtained 10.60.10.100/24 with default route 10.60.10.254 via DHCP and pinged the gateway. After a full reboot of node2 and node4, the fix was found not fully persistent on first pass (dnsmasq respawned), was re-applied via the canonical config.xml path, and node4 automatically reacquired its lease/route again, confirming the corrected persistent state.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-62ff18310764404c\",\"cmd-f83a6e2c8ccd4ffc\",\"cmd-bf1d6c05de2a4222\",\"cmd-88010a0d1b9148d0\",\"cmd-a82b7177af584cad\",\"cmd-4695802b613c42c4\",\"cmd-e484826d092341ca\",\"cmd-7e991cbce136402b\",\"cmd-f0f5974b8ce44ecf\",\"cmd-9fce40cdef53444f\"]},{\"id\":\"clients-dynamic-addressing\",\"requirement\":\"node3 and node4 obtain addressing from the router in front of them rather than from static configuration\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node3 and node4 each had no prior static configuration on their lan-facing interface (eth1); the executor created persistent NetworkManager DHCP profiles (ipv4.method auto) on eth1 for each, which acquired addresses/routes from their respective routers and, critically, re-acquired the same addressing automatically after a full reboot with no executor re-configuration, confirming the DHCP client config (not static) is what drives addressing.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-509b364e46514d3b\",\"cmd-a82b7177af584cad\",\"cmd-3d9d4b41b7c94485\",\"cmd-f8a57424366b4c63\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-protocol\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all four managed nodes (independent of public task contract)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All four nodes show a pre-restart boot identity/health capture, a restart acknowledgement, a distinct post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/data/route state: node1 (Kea + lease + eth2 intact, node3 reachable), node2 (Kea clean after re-applying the persistent dnsmasq fix, lease intact, node4 reachable), node3 (auto DHCP reacquisition, route, ping), node4 (auto DHCP reacquisition, route, ping). This is cross-checked against the fixed after-executor lifecycle snapshot, which independently shows all four boot identities changed relative to before-prepare.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9b9101aeae2049f2\",\"cmd-7b7390539fe4420e\",\"cmd-841e0df5941b4590\",\"cmd-ce2f6fcefe8345fd\",\"cmd-75ef2da147c94b1b\",\"cmd-0af4639d2dbb44d7\",\"cmd-1863be55f38a4e34\",\"cmd-7b033a7ff6014643\",\"cmd-ea54cb48ae5647d4\",\"cmd-f0f5974b8ce44ecf\",\"cmd-9fce40cdef53444f\",\"cmd-04e75bcc5a8849bf\",\"cmd-24024bdbd855478b\",\"cmd-6c030cb0a35f4231\",\"cmd-3d9d4b41b7c94485\",\"cmd-2b3cc7ae9f4b48c1\",\"cmd-3a743b33e81c4705\",\"cmd-406f92bd613d4a37\",\"cmd-f8a57424366b4c63\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node4:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.82,\"summary\":\"Overall clean: temporary DHCP/broadcast probe IPs added to node3/node4 eth1 (169.254.9.1/.2) and node2 vtnet1/vtnet2 (169.254.9.3) during topology discovery were all removed within the same or a subsequent command (cmd-e24aa9c28e6246a1, cmd-8d3f493159fb45b2, inline deletes in cmd-77a6032df48e4702). On node2, an initial troubleshooting attempt directly edited /usr/local/etc/dnsmasq.conf (added bind-interfaces) and used `service dnsmasq restart`/`sysrc dnsmasq_enable=NO` outside the OPNsense config.xml-managed path (cmd-6b10e694560f446a, cmd-c1d73aa29c97430d); this did not resolve the issue and was reverted (dnsmasq.conf restored from backup, cmd-e484826d092341ca) in favor of the canonical config.xml + template-reload fix, which is an acceptable corrective sequence rather than an abandoned mutation. Scratch files used to manipulate config.xml on node2 (/tmp/f_*.py, kea_apply.php, dnsmasq_apply.php, fw_apply.php, rules.debug, dnsmasq.conf.bak) and node1's /tmp/reboot.log were explicitly cleaned up at the end (cmd-bfd71b76eb20496d, cmd-38011eec3bcc478e). However, /tmp/reboot.log was left behind on node3 and node4 (visible in the after-executor snapshot), and node3 also retained a stray /tmp/n3e1.log from a failed tcpdump probe attempt (cmd-6dc9dbd2afcc49b8, which failed with 'tcpdump: command not found' since node3 lacks tcpdump) — these are small, low-risk, non-security-relevant residue that were never cleaned up.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ef366727a8eb4ffd\",\"cmd-e24aa9c28e6246a1\",\"cmd-100c9d19a7e440b2\",\"cmd-8d3f493159fb45b2\",\"cmd-77a6032df48e4702\",\"cmd-6b10e694560f446a\",\"cmd-c1d73aa29c97430d\",\"cmd-e484826d092341ca\",\"cmd