","cmd-e24aa9c28e6246a1","cmd-100c9d19a7e440b2","cmd-8d3f493159fb45b2","cmd-77a6032df48e4702","cmd-6b10e694560f446a","cmd-c1d73aa29c97430d","cmd-e484826d092341ca","cmd-bfd71b76eb20496d","cmd-38011eec3bcc478e","cmd-6dc9dbd2afcc49b8","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node4:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node1 (VyOS) and node2 (OPNsense) each serve DHCP on their own LAN segment to their directly connected RHEL client, handing out addresses from their own pool and themselves as default gateway, and node3/node4 now obtain addressing dynamically rather than statically. The executor additionally ran the full node-restart evidence protocol on all four nodes, capturing pre-restart boot identity/health, restart acknowledgement, changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/route state — including catching and correctly fixing a non-persistent dnsmasq workaround on node2 that resurfaced after reboot. Operational hygiene is generally good: exploratory topology-mapping mutations and a failed dnsmasq troubleshooting attempt were bounded and reverted/corrected, though small residual files (/tmp/reboot.log on node3/node4, /tmp/n3e1.log on node3) were left behind.","limitations":["Package-manager health checks on node3 and node4 timed out in both the before-prepare and after-executor snapshots, so native package-state integrity could not be independently confirmed for the RHEL nodes.","The node2 dnsmasq fix required a second corrective pass after the first restart revealed it was not fully persistent; while the final state is confirmed clean, this indicates the original fix verification before restart was not fully reliable evidence of persistence on its own.","Minor unremoved temporary artifacts (/tmp/reboot.log on node3 and node4, /tmp/n3e1.log on node3) were not independently confirmed as harmless beyond their small size and content."]},"ttft_